title: "TESS transit candidates, three further results and notable eclipsing binaries, 2026"
version: "v1.3.2"
type: dataset
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23453967
authors:
  - family-names: Zarco
    given-names: A.
date-released: "2026-10-20"
repository-code: "https://github.com/alejandrozarco/tess-candidates-2026"
abstract: >-
  Transit timing, light-curve tables, false-positive checks, TRICERATOPS runs and predicted transit times for five
  transit candidates found in TESS full-frame images: TIC 402710280 (P = 36.34005 d, depth about 1680 ppm,
  transit-timing variations up to 29 min), TIC 324429952 (P = 42.37574 d, depth about 1500 ppm), TIC 89247735
  (P = 18.32317 d, dilution-corrected depth 1.13-1.22 %, Rp about 8.7 Earth radii with the stellar radius from an SED
  fit), TIC 455017950 (HD 134147; P = 40.60565 d, depth about 1310 ppm, Rp about 3.2 Earth radii) and TIC 311179599
  (M0 dwarf at 47.5 pc; P = 24.82684 d, depth about 1435 ppm, Rp about 2.5 Earth radii; one transit 2.2 h from the
  linear ephemeris of the other seven, a timing variation not established; a candidate second transit signal from
  three events, about 880 ppm, Rp about 2.0 Earth radii, P = 73.8672 d or 36.9336 d). Three further
  results: the eclipsing binary TIC 11613676 (CD-30 448; P = 12.1158 d), whose eclipses were absent in TESS sector 3
  (2018), present in sector 30 (2020), 5.4 % deep in sector 97 (2025) and shallower in 2026; and TOI-540 b, whose
  transits are not detected in TESS sector 98 (2025-2026). Nine eclipsing binaries without a previous eclipse report:
  TIC 13957664, a single eclipse at the conjunction predicted by its Gaia DR3 astrometric+spectroscopic orbit
  (P = 245.66 d, i = 91.1 +- 0.8 deg); TIC 304936165, an eccentric 61.7-d Gaia SB1 with one eclipse per orbit; the
  eclipsing Gaia SB1s TIC 120522352, TIC 382523691, TIC 261702897 and TIC 265436769; CPD-62 2890 (TIC 433642561) in
  the open cluster NGC 4609; the 1.7075-d eclipsing binary TIC 245068282 in a hierarchical triple; and
  TIC 342194997 (TYC 9251-1375-1), a known Gaia DR3 astrometric+spectroscopic binary (P = 550.75 d, e = 0.84) with a
  single eclipse by its M-dwarf companion at the predicted conjunction. TIC 124751008 (2MASS J06162084-3509125), a
  12.61-d eclipsing binary in an M-dwarf multiple at 57 pc, whose primary eclipses were 2.25 % deep in 2018, 0.28 % in
  2024-2025 and 0.77 % in 2026, with eclipse-timing variations; the geometric models require a change of the orbital
  inclination. Not peer reviewed; the candidates are not confirmed.
